  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...A few spotty showers and thunderstorms today.\n\nAs the next shortwave trough moves into the eastern Great Lakes \nregion, moisture will increase today. Diurnal heating combined with \nlake breeze and terrain effects will support a chance for a few \nscattered showers and thunderstorms across the inland portions of \nthe area this afternoon and into the early evening. High-res \nguidance continues to support an area of numerous showers and \nthunderstorms across the metro area of Toronto to move across the \nNiagara Peninsula and into the Buffalo/Niagara Falls metros this \nevening.\n\n\nKey Message 2...More unsettled weather will return Monday and \nTuesday with increasing coverage of showers and thunderstorms. \n\nA broad longwave trough will remain overhead of the region Monday \nand Tuesday, while a large upper level ridge to the east of the \ntrough, will allow multiple shortwave trough to ripple through the \nlongwave pattern. The shortwave disturbances combined with the \nseasonably warm air, will result in unsettled weather with rounds of \nshowers and scattered thunderstorms.\n\nAs an initial shortwave trough passes over the area during the time \nof peak diurnal heating and instability, showers and scattered \nstorms will form inland from the lakes Monday afternoon, especially \nacross the the Finger Lakes and into the eastern Lake Ontario region \nwhere there will be more available moisture and the maxima of \nforcing from the passing shortwave will overlap. Given the timing of \nthe shortwave passage being during peak heating, there is a Marginal \nRisk for Severe Weather Monday due to the weak low to mid level \nwinds and weak shear across the region. Additionally given the weak \nflow, localized heavy downpours will be the main concern, due to \nPWAT values of around 1.5 inches. However, deep-layer shear of 25 to \n30 knots across the western Southern Tier may support a couple to a \nfew stronger storms.\n\nAnother stronger shortwave trough and associated surface wave will \npass southeast across the Great Lakes Monday night and into Tuesday \nsupporting more widespread showers and scattered thunderstorms, with \nthe best coverage occuring Tuesday afternoon and evening correlating \nto the timing of diurnal heating/destabilization effects. Shear \nprofiles continue to be weak, and do not support organized severe \nstorms, however across the Southern Tier there may be enough shear \nto support a few stray showers across the Ohio Valley and Mid-\nAtlantic to become severe. Otherwise, a soupy airmass overhead (PWAT \nvalues of around 1.5 inches or slightly higher, will support a few \nheavy downpours.\n\nKey Message 3...Showers and storms will linger into Wednesday, with \ndrier conditions for the end of the work week.\n\nIn the wake of the passing system Tuesday, lingering longwave \ntroughing will remain across the region, supporting another round of \ndiurnally driven convention to develop inland from the lakes \nWednesday. However due to the notably weaker instability Wednesday, \nmost of the activity will be constrained to brief and numerous \nshowers with a few widely scattered thunderstorms.\n\nOtherwise, surface high pressure will lift northeast across eastern \nCanada supporting a generally dry trend for the remainder of the \nweek.",
